\begin{proposition}[automatic local complex compatibility]\label{maps:src:F.2.15}
\StatementRecord{maps:src:F.2.15}{proved}{Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), effort high, 16 November 2026; also checked in this writing by \CurrentChecker}{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; \cref{maps:src:F.2.7}; \cref{maps:src:F.1.4}}


Every simply connected datum of F.2 with two finite affine
fillings automatically has finite linear actions commuting
with real complex structures.  This assertion concerns the
individual finite actions and does not produce simultaneous
global periods.

\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[simultaneous integral conjugacy for every input]\label{maps:src:F.2.36}
\StatementRecord{maps:src:F.2.36}{proved}{Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), effort high, 16 November 2026; also checked in this writing by \CurrentChecker}{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; \cite[Algorithm 8.3, p.~21]{rootBHJ22}; \cite[Algorithm 8.3 and its correctness proof, p.~21]{rootBHJ22}; \cite[Theorem 3.4, p.~6; Propositions 8.1--8.2, p.~21]{rootBHJ22}}


There is an unconditional exact algorithm which decides simultaneous
$\mathrm{GL}_6(\mathbb Z)$ conjugacy of any two marked matrix tuples of
F.2 and returns a conjugating matrix when one exists. The algorithm
does not impose a bound on their entries, on the affine multiplicities,
or on the integral conjugator. More generally it applies to any integral
tuple containing a matrix $1+N$ with $N^2=0$ and $\operatorname{rank}N=3$;
the primitive-image assumption is unnecessary for this statement.

\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{night:P1B.2}
\StatementRecord{night:P1B.2}{proved}{S6\_06\_BLIND: GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI, ChatGPT Pro mode, effort Pro), recorded re-derivation in the checkpoint dated 18 November 2026; also checked in this writing by \CurrentChecker}{Definitions and the displayed constructions}
A finite-order integral linear action on $\R^6$ admitting a
free finite affine torus lift has characteristic polynomial
among exactly thirty-eight cyclotomic products. Their linear
orders are
\[
 1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12.
\]
Exactly nineteen of these polynomials admit a commuting complex
structure on $\R^6$. These are a preliminary spectral list;
no assertion about global gluings is inferred from the list.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[exclusion of the abelian-surface source]\label{maps:src:F.3.4}
\StatementRecord{maps:src:F.3.4}{proved}{GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI, ChatGPT Pro mode), effort Pro, 2026-11-17; Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), effort high, 2026-11-16; also checked in this writing by \CurrentChecker}{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; \cite[Definition 4.2.4 and Proposition 4.2.5, p.~44; Theorem 4.2.6, p.~45]{ffDeligne}; \cref{maps:src:F.3.1}}


The Engel construction proposed in F.3, starting with a polarized
algebraic family of abelian surfaces with a toric-rank-two cusp,
taking a fibrewise degree-zero line bundle, and quotienting its
punctured bundle by a lifted translation, cannot have $b_1=b_2=0$
with the stipulated rank-three square-zero Kuhn cusp.
The two sections need not be proportional.

\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[the unpolarized period domain has LSAP]\label{maps:src:F.3.8}
\StatementRecord{maps:src:F.3.8}{proved}{GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI, ChatGPT Pro mode), effort Pro, 2026-11-17; Claude Fable 5.2 (Anthropic), effort high, 16 November 2026; also checked in this writing by \CurrentChecker}{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; \cite[Definition 2.1, p.~4]{IVVOkaOneV5}; \cite[Proposition 7.13 and preceding paragraph, pp.~23--24]{IVVOkaOneV5}; \cref{maps:eq:F.O3}; \cref{maps:eq:F.O4}}


Let $D_3$ be the component of
\[
 \{F\in\operatorname{Gr}(3,\mathbb C^6):F\cap\overline F=0\}
\]
containing the standard complex structure, so that
$D_3\cong GL^+(6,\mathbb R)/GL(3,\mathbb C)$.
At every point, $D_3$
is dominated by a spanning tube of nine complex lines.  In particular,
it has the local spray approximation property (LSAP) and is Oka--1.
These assertions concern the unpolarized domain.

\end{theorem}
